  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The third conduit fractured along its grain at the six-hour mark, splitting with a dry snap that sent the fire-core skittering across the workbench. ☽'tenebris caught it before it hit the floor — reflex, not thought — and held the crystal between her scarred flippers, reading its remaining heat by the color at its center. Pale amber shading to grey at the edges. Four hours of charge left in a core that had started the evening at full burn.
    
    Six hours. The bone-lattice had been filed to tolerance, the grain aligned with the thermal axis, the Star∴eternal channel seated cleanly in the marrow-thread. She had done everything correctly, and the conduit had cracked because fire-core heat expanded the bone at a rate the lattice could not absorb. Every practitioner who had tried to extend a core's burn cycle had hit the same wall. The bone conducted. The bone expanded. The bone broke.
    
    She set the fractured conduit beside its predecessors — nine of them now, split along different axes, each failure annotated in the shorthand she kept scratched into the workbench slate. Behind her, the fire-core stockpile sat in its stone alcove: forty-one cores remaining, down from sixty at the start of the season. The count-marks on the alcove wall told the story better than any ledger. Courier schedules from the upper tunnels had grown erratic. The last shipment from ☽'shadow Umbrsulcavus~ had been fourteen cores short of manifest.
    
    The workshop at Sleek☽ Caemomot was not a room anyone had intended. It was a junction where three architectural periods collided — the Shadow Covens' original high-ceilinged excavation meeting the Exchange's crude expansion tunnel meeting a natural fissure that neither had planned for. The ceiling seams didn't match. The floor sloped differently on each side. Light from the braziers puddled in wrong places, gathering in corners that should have been dark and leaving shadowed the workbench where ☽'tenebris needed to see.
    
    And across the workshop, behind its case of volcanic glass, the Swaleed~ Luminary burned.
    
    Its glass was ugly — three wars of fracture layered over one another, the hairline frost-wail cracks from Schism threading beneath the long jagged scar of the Bra∴conflictu and the newer concentric spiderweb radiating from a point near the base. Through all that damage, the flame burned on. Blue-dark, almost the color of absence. A flame the size of a flipper-joint, throwing heat that could be felt at four body-lengths — impossible for a core that size, a core that had been burning since before ☽'tenebris's grandmother entered the tunnels. She had measured its radiant output eleven times, compared it against every grading standard the Collective maintained. The numbers contradicted themselves: the core should have exhausted generations ago, yet its output exceeded a fresh Class III.
    
    You could not simply make bone hold more fire. The material refused. What the Luminary's maker had understood instead — that was the question.
    
    The workshop air carried the mineral sharpness of overworked fire-cores and the deeper, wetter smell of the northern wall — the seepage. Bioluminescent fungi climbed the corrupted ice-stone in slow blooms, their blue-sick glow pulsing faintly with a rhythm that did not correspond to any draft or temperature change ☽'tenebris had been able to isolate. The seepage wept in thin rivulets along channels older than the Collective, pooling in depressions worn smooth by centuries of traffic. The ice here remembered, and what it remembered was heat.
    
    She selected a fresh length of bone-lattice stock from the rack — narwhal-sourced, tight-grained, the kind that held a channel without splintering for at least twelve hours under standard thermal load. She clamped it in the filing vice and began cutting the conduit groove with a fire-tooth rasp, angling the stroke to follow the grain's natural curve. The rasp bit cleanly. Bone-dust collected in the catch-tray, fine as snow, smelling faintly of char where the rasp's heated edge scorched the shavings.
    
    She seated a Class II core into the receiving socket. The bone warmed immediately — heat crawling along the grain, following the channel she'd cut, distributing itself with the particular evenness that meant the lattice was well-aligned. She fed Star∴eternal into the connection: captured fire flowing through bone, threading the marrow with ash-light until the conduit glowed translucent orange along its length. The sound was a low, sustained hiss, like steam through a fissure. The smell was hot mineral and something older, something that reminded her of the deep forges at Thrall☽ Vavmastius where the technique had been born.
    
    Hour one. The bone held. Hour three. Hairline fractures appeared at the socket join — thermal expansion pressing against the lattice's grain structure. By hour five, the conduit was singing. A thin, high tone that meant the bone was stress-loaded past its recovery threshold. She noted the pitch, noted the fracture pattern, noted that the heat distribution had shifted — concentrating at the mid-point rather than spreading evenly.
    
    She moved the apparatus closer to the bench's edge to make room for documentation, and the conduit's singing changed.
    
    Lower. Steadier. The hairline fractures stopped propagating.
    
    The northern wall was three flippers' breadth away. The seepage had crept along the stone surface toward the conduit's warmth, a thin film of bioluminescent moisture pooling against the base of the vice. Where the seepage touched the bench-stone beneath the conduit, the fungi were growing — accelerated, visibly thickening, their blue glow brightening in response to the fire-core's heat.
    
    The conduit's thermal reading had dropped by two degrees. The core's color had not changed.
    
    She pulled the apparatus back to the bench's center. The singing returned. The fractures resumed their slow advance. By hour seven, the conduit split. She set it with the others and wrote in her slate: *proximity to seepage — thermal bleed or thermal exchange?*
    
    Then she cleaned her tools and went to find Cevmucsiatum-larv~.
    
    He was in the lower market, his leather satchel unmarked, his flippers flat on the table in the studied visibility that had become his only concession to what the ice had shown him. The cracked fire-core amulet hung at his throat, its light long dead. He did not look up when she sat down.
    
    "I need you to read the deep-ice behind the northern wall," she said. "The memory-layers. Thermal impressions from the original workshop."
    
    His left flipper tremored against the table surface. "The ice remembers what the ledger forgets."
    
    "The Luminary's maker worked somewhere in those tunnels. The substrate will have recorded the process-heat. I need to see how the original conduits were seated — what they were seated *in*."
    
    "That ice will push back."
    
    "I know what it costs. I'm asking."
    
    He turned the cracked amulet between his flippers. Then he went.
    
    She watched the tremor climb from his left flipper into his shoulder as he pressed his weight against the northern wall and read what the frozen stone remembered. Thermal ghosts unspooled in the blue-sick light: the impression of a workbench positioned *against* the weeping stone, not away from it. Conduits seated in housings carved from corrupted ice-stone itself. Fire-core heat flowing into bone-lattice and the bone-lattice bleeding excess into the seepage — the fungi metabolizing it, their bioluminescent chemistry converting thermal energy into something the substrate could hold without expansion stress.
    
    Not a seal against heat loss. A *valve*. The bone never reached fracture stress because the thermal load was continuously siphoned into the living substrate. The fungi fed on what should kill them, and in feeding, they kept the system in equilibrium.
    
    The flame that feeds on what should kill it.
    
    She built the housing from corrupted ice-stone cut directly from the northern wall. The stone wept as she carved it — thin luminescent tears that smelled of deep-pressure and organic chemistry and ages of slow metabolic patience. She lined the interior cavity with bone-lattice, but this time she did not seal the grain against moisture. She left the lattice porous, open to the seepage's touch, and filled the channels with living fungal culture scraped from the oldest growths near the ceiling where the corruption ran deepest.
    
    The fire-core seated into the lattice with a click she felt through her scarred flippers. She channeled Star∴eternal — careful, controlled, feeding captured fire through the bone at the standard rate. The marrow-threads lit with ash-light. The conduit warmed. And the fungi began to glow.
    
    Not the sickly blue of passive growth. Brighter. Warmer. The bioluminescent chemistry responding to the heat, accelerating, weaving itself through the bone's open grain like thread through mesh. The thermal readings steadied. The bone did not sing. The lattice absorbed fire-core heat and bled the excess into the living fungal network, which metabolized it, which grew, which bled residual light back into the stone — down through the housing, into the weeping wall's seepage, into the deep ice.
    
    A cycle. Closed and self-sustaining.
    
    She watched for nine hours. The conduit did not fracture. The fire-core did not dim. The temperature held steady within a range so narrow she checked the measuring-crystal twice.
    
    Cevmucsiatum-larv~ came when she sent for him. He stood in the workshop doorway and looked at the conduit glowing against the north wall, its bone-lattice threaded with blue-lit fungal veins, the fire-core burning amber and steady inside its nest of corruption. His left flipper shook. He pressed it against his coat and held it still by force.
    
    "How long?" he asked.
    
    "Twelve hours. Same color. Same output."
    
    He came closer despite himself. The Swaleed~ Luminary sat on the bench beside the new apparatus, its ancient dark-blue flame burning in its scarred glass. The new conduit glowed warmer, younger — the amber of a core that had just been lit, except it had been burning for twelve hours at a rate that should have killed it in seven.
    
    "Vaulius-phantasm~ built the Luminary into corrupted glass," he said quietly. "The corruption is what kept it alive."
    
    ☽'tenebris said nothing. She was watching the fungi grow along the conduit's grain, slow and steady, their blue light warming toward something that was not quite gold.
    
    The amber glow reflected off the weeping wall and the fungi bloomed around it, reaching, feeding, fed. The tremor in his left flipper slowed, and he let it hang at his side without bracing it.
    
    "The ice remembers what the ledger forgets," he said softly, and for once it didn't sound like a boundary.
    
    ☽'tenebris sat with the fire-core's steadiness for another moment. Then she went back to the bench, picked up her rasp, and reached for a fresh length of bone-lattice stock.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771734585564_0", anchor: "the Shadow Covens' original high-ceilinged excavation meeting the Exchange's crude expansion tunnel", text: "The chronicler has independently confirmed my architectural sequence. See my annotation on Sleek☽ Caemomot's entry: four administrations, zero surviving charters, walls that remember everything. Here the walls do more than remember. They participate.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771734585564_1", anchor: "forty-one cores remaining, down from sixty at the start of the season", text: "Sixty. A round number at the season's start. Either the Collective stocks in multiples of ten — possible, given Nightshelf accounting customs — or the figure was reconstructed afterward. I note it and move on.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771734585564_2", anchor: "His left flipper tremored against the table surface", text: "In my annotation on his entry, I described a constellation of symptoms and stopped one sentence short of a word I was not prepared to write. He reads the deep ice and it pushes back. She asks him to do it knowing what it costs. He goes. This is the part of the archive that defeats me — not the grand betrayals or the faction wars but the small moments where someone does what hurts them because someone they trust has asked.", type: :tangent, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771734585564_3", anchor: "the fungi metabolizing it, their bioluminescent chemistry converting thermal energy into something the substrate could hold without expansion stress", text: "Every corruption report I have filed describes the seepage as contamination — destabilizing substrate, compromising structural integrity, category: threat. The deep ice at comparable strata records the same fungi in symbiosis with thermal vents that predate penguin habitation by epochs. The corruption was never a disease. It was an ecology we interrupted. ☽'tenebris did not invent this technique. She recovered it from something older than us that had been growing on the wall the entire time, waiting for someone to stop flinching.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771734585564_4", anchor: "Vaulius-phantasm~ built the Luminary into corrupted glass", text: "This name appears in no Foundation Depths record I have located. Either the ice-memory supplied it directly during Cevmucsiatum-larv~'s reading — plausible, given the thermal impression's depth and clarity — or he already knew it from Collective oral tradition and the ice confirmed what the ledgers never held. Either way: the first attribution of the Luminary's maker in any source. It should not be buried in dialogue.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771734585564_5", anchor: "The tremor in his left flipper slowed", text: "Too neat. Too kind. But I have seen the ice do stranger things to those who give it what it asks for.", type: :tangent, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771734585564_6", anchor: "picked up her rasp, and reached for a fresh length of bone-lattice stock", text: "She has solved the fundamental constraint of fire-core longevity — the problem that is, even as I write this, slowly killing the Collective, the Accord, and possibly both civilizations — and her immediate response is to build another one. To replicate. To confirm. The Clever Ice Age in a single gesture.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
